\details{
Membership is by data value, so it follows the scale rather than the drawn
shapes:
\itemize{
\item \strong{continuous} (including \code{log10}, \code{sqrt} and \code{reverse} axes): the element's
value lies in the brushed data range;
\item \strong{date / datetime}: the same test on the date's numeric value;
\item \strong{discrete}: the element's category is one whose band centre the brush
covers.
}

With \code{encodings = c("x", "y")} (the default) the brush is a rectangle and both
values are tested; with \code{"x"} it spans the panel's full height and only the x
value is tested; \code{"y"} is the transpose. In a faceted plot the data range applies to every
panel, so brushing one facet selects matching rows in all of them.

The selection lives in the plot spec, so it is \strong{inert on a static render}
and is enacted by a capable host (\code{vellumwidget::as_widget()}) without Shiny.
Under Shiny the members are reported in \verb{input$<id>_selected} and the brushed
data range in \verb{input$<id>_brush}.

Like \code{\link[=select_point]{select_point()}}, it is pipe-first (attach to the plot and return it) or
free-standing (call with the name as the first argument, then share it across
